---
title: Testing
summary: Use the devtools and the profiler from your own tests, so Playwright can measure hydration, interactions, server time and page weight and fail when a budget is missed.
related:
  - devtools/overview
  - profiler/overview
---

A performance problem that you find by hand in the devtools tends to come back a few releases later, because nothing checks for it. `@ogygia/testing` lets your own tests use what the devtools and the profiler measure. Your tests click through the app as they already do, ogygia measures what happened, and the test asserts on the result. It gives you four primitives:

- `page()`: the page load as the browser measured it, including vitals, each island's wake, and the findings of the [Page tab](/docs/devtools/overview#the-page-tab).
- `hydration()`: each island's hydration status, with the difference for one that changed (the [Hydration tab](/docs/devtools/overview#the-hydration-tab)).
- `record(actions)`: what your actions did, including slow interactions and the function behind each, dead and repeated clicks, requests with the server's own breakdown, long tasks, shifts and errors (the [Record tab](/docs/devtools/overview#recording-a-session)).
- `profile(path)`: the [SSR profiler](/docs/profiler/overview)'s report for a path, with score, forecast, findings and weight.

## With Playwright

Install the package next to Playwright. It is a test helper rather than a plugin, so nothing goes into `vite.config.ts` for it.

```bash
pnpm add -D @ogygia/testing @playwright/test
```

ogygia does not import Playwright itself, so you hand it your own `test` and `expect`:

```ts title=tests/fixtures.ts
import { test as base, expect as baseExpect } from '@playwright/test';
import { withOgygia } from '@ogygia/testing';

export const { test, expect } = withOgygia(base, baseExpect);
```

```ts title=tests/checkout.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';

test('checkout stays fast', async ({ page, ogygia }) => {
  await page.goto('/cart');

  expect(await ogygia.page()).toMeetBudget({ lcp: 2500, cls: 0.1 });
  expect(await ogygia.hydration()).toHydrateCleanly();

  const session = await ogygia.record(async () => {
    await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Buy' }).click();
  });
  expect(session).toHaveNoFindings({ severity: 'warn' });
  expect(session).toMeetBudget({ maxInteractionMs: 200, errors: 0 });

  expect(await ogygia.profile('/cart')).toMeetBudget({ score: 80, renderMs: 200 });
});
```

The server under test needs the [`devtools()` plugin](/docs/devtools/overview#enabling) listed, which means either the dev server or a preview build with devtools on (see [Devtools in a build](/docs/devtools/overview#enabling-in-a-build-a-preview-deploy)). The fixture turns devtools on (with the `og_devtools` cookie) for your `baseURL`, or, if there is no `baseURL`, for the origin of the first page the test opens, so each page is measured from its first load. `profile()` also needs `devtools({ profiler: true })`, and on a build you give it the profiler's key:

```ts title=playwright.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  use: { baseURL: 'http://127.0.0.1:4173', ogygiaOptions: { profilerKey: process.env.OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET } },
});
```

A recording can span pages. Actions that cause a full page load (`page.goto`, a form post, a plain link) keep recording on the next page, and the report covers all of them (`session.counts.pages`). If the actions leave for a document that has no testing API (another site, or a page without devtools), the recording is lost, and `record()` says so with your actions' own error as its `cause`.

To stay on the page instead, record with `blockNavigation`. Every document load the actions cause (a reload in a handler, a stray link) is answered with `204`, so the browser stays where it is, and each one is listed:

```ts
const session = await ogygia.record(() => page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Filter' }).click(), { blockNavigation: true });
session.navigations; // [{ url: 'https://…/products', after: 'button.filter "Filter"', island: 'ProductFilter', t: 4120, approx: false }]
```

`after` is what started the navigation, as seen when it began: the link or form, or the input being handled at the time (a reload inside a click handler names that button). Where the browser cannot tell (it has no Navigation API, which is Chromium-only) or the site runs an older engine, `approx` is `true` and `after` is only the last input before the navigation. `navigations` is the list to count and compare. Each entry also appears as a `navigation-blocked` finding for reading, which `compare` leaves out.

Each island in `hydration()` has a `key` for its instance, so a diff can say which carousel broke: `Carousel#2` is the second island of that name in page order. That number shifts when an island is added earlier on the page. For a key that survives such changes, give the fixture `ogygiaOptions: { islandKey: 'data-block-id' }`, and each island is keyed by that attribute on itself or on its nearest ancestor that has it, across shadow roots (so an island inside a web component finds the block around the component). Two islands under the same keyed element become `id` and `id#2`. Each island also carries `t0` and `done`, the times when it began to wake and when it could respond, in ms on the page's clock, so a single report tells you which step of a session woke it. Session findings carry `names`, the islands they point at, as page findings do.

### Changed, but how?

A `changed` island says what kind of change it was. `attributesOnly: true` means the elements and text are the same and only attributes differ; they are listed in `attributes` as `{ tag, name, from, to }`. Often that is not the app rendering differently, but a web component setting its own `tabindex` or `aria-*` as it upgrades, or an inline style being set and cleared. `inputDuringHydration: true` means a pointer, a key press or focus landed on the island while it hydrated, so its DOM may have responded to that input. You can let either case through in a matcher, or leave chosen attribute changes out of the report altogether:

```ts
expect(await ogygia.hydration()).toHydrateCleanly({ allowAttributeChanges: true, allowInput: true });

// playwright.config.ts: these read `clean`, with what was left out in `ignored`
use: { ogygiaOptions: { ignoreAttributes: [{ tag: /^qds-/, name: /^(tabindex|aria-)/ }, { name: 'style', value: '' }] } }
```

A rule matches the element's tag, the attribute's name and its new value (or its old value when the attribute was removed). A string matches exactly, and a RegExp matches by `test`.

### What measuring costs, and lean testing

Measuring has a cost. The devtools check each island's markup as it hydrates, build their report and load the testing engine, none of which a visitor with devtools off pays for. `page().self` tells you what it cost: `script_ms` is the main-thread time spent on ogygia's own measuring so far (broken down by `phases`), and `bytes` is the decoded size of the testing engine's files. Subtract both from any CPU or JS-weight measurement.

Lean testing reduces the cost further. With `ogygiaOptions: { lean: true }` (which sets the `og_testing=lean` cookie next to `og_devtools=on`, or use `?og-testing=lean`), the page runs the testing API and nothing else from the devtools: no launcher, no dock, no watcher of its timers and no event side channel in its HTML. It loads what it would load with devtools off, plus the testing engine when a test first asks for it. Findings that need the server's events are not available in this mode. Every report says which mode ran (`engine.lean`), and an engine older than version 3 ignores the cookie and runs the full devtools.

### When the site runs an older ogygia

The reports are built by the page's testing engine, which ships with the site's build rather than with your test's `ogygia` package. A check that measures an older deploy (a baseline) next to a new preview therefore gets older reports from the baseline. Every report says which engine answered (`report.engine.version`, where 3 is current), and the first older report logs one warning per site for the whole run. Whatever the test side knows is filled in: `navigations` from the loads it held back (with `approx: true`), and `names: []` on findings. Instance keys and `counts.cls` are missing from older reports, so `compare` then counts hydration by island name and skips the session's CLS rather than report a change that did not happen.

What each engine version detects:

| Version   | Added                                                                                            | Detection                                                                                                           |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1 (0.8.2) |                                                                                                  |                                                                                                                     |
| 2         | instance `key`, `t0` / `done`, `navigations`, finding `names`, `counts.cls`                      | A router navigation that never swapped the page no longer starts a new page for `page()`. Hydration status as in 1. |
| 3         | `attributesOnly`, `attributes`, `inputDuringHydration`, `ignoreAttributes`, `self`, lean testing | Hydration status as in 1 and 2. The new fields only say what kind of change it was.                                 |

Since hydration status is detected the same way in every version, an island that reads `clean` on one engine and `changed` on another really did differ in that run. Input landing while it hydrated is the usual cause, and version 3 flags it.

## The server, over the same actions

`record(actions, { server: true })` also records the server while your actions run, capturing the profiler's CPU across every request they made, their outbound calls, and the requests themselves. The result lands on the session as `server`:

```ts
const session = await ogygia.record(() => page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Buy' }).click(), { server: true });

session.server.window.requests; // [{ method: 'POST', path: '/cart/checkout', ms: 412, status: 200 }, …]
expect(session.server).toHaveNoFindings({ severity: 'warn' });
expect(session).toMeetBudget({ serverBusyMs: 300 });
```

Under the hood this uses the profiler's window: `POST /__profiler/window/start` opens it, and `POST /__profiler/window/stop` answers with the report. Only one window runs at a time. It waits a few seconds for a background sample to finish, and stops itself after a minute.

On a serverless deployment, the start, the session's requests and the stop can each land on a different instance. If you give the profiler a store (redis, postgres or SQLite; see [the storage interface](/docs/api/devtools-profiler-storage)), it works anyway:

- Start answers with a signed window id, and the driver adds it to each of the session's requests to the site (`x-og-window`).
- Every instance writes such a request, with its own `cpu_ms`, to the store before it answers.
- Stop, on any instance, gathers them, and `window.instances` says how many instances served the session.
- The CPU profile comes from the instance that started the window, since sampling is per process, so a stop that lands elsewhere returns the requests without it.

A window that cannot run never costs you the session; `session.server` is then `{ error }`.

## Profiles for a CI check

- **`engine.version`:** a profile names the ogygia version that produced it, the same number the page reports use. Its detectors change with that version, so compare profiles only within one version. A profile from 0.8.2 has no `engine`.
- **`profile(path, { fields: ['requests', 'network', 'findings', 'assets', 'summary'] })`:** returns only those sections. A full report is hundreds of KB, mostly `stacks`, `hot_functions` and `drill`.
- **`?weigh=full`:** weighs every file the page loads without a time budget (on a serverless host, within whatever time the request has left), so `assets.totals` stays stable from run to run. Each file it could not weigh says why in `assets.missed_why`: `budget`, `timeout`, `status 404`, `file cap` or `error`.
- **`Document-Policy: js-profiling`:** a page with devtools switched on is sent this header, so the browser's CPU sampler runs without the app having to send it.
- **`page().route`:** the SvelteKit route id the server rendered, on a build with devtools on.
- **More CPU functions:** `ogygiaOptions: { cpuFunctions: 50 }` keeps more than the default 15 entries in `page().cpu.fns`.
- **Session lists:** `session.failed` lists every request that answered with an error status. `session.by_element` gives the slowest interaction on each element the session touched, with 0 for a click too quick for the browser to time.

## The matchers

| Matcher                                    | Passes when                                                                                                                            |
| ------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `toHaveNoFindings({ severity?, ignore? })` | No finding at or above `severity` (`info`, `warn`, `error`), except the codes in `ignore`. Works on page, session and profile reports. |
| `toMeetBudget(budget)`                     | Every line of the budget is met.                                                                                                       |
| `toHydrateCleanly({ allow? })`             | No island failed, recovered, or changed its markup (`allow` lets some statuses through).                                               |

A failing matcher says what missed and where: `Clock: changed — −rendered on the server +rendered on the browser`.

The budget lines available for each report:

| Report      | Lines                                                                                                                                                |
| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `page()`    | `lcp`, `fcp`, `cls`, `inp`, `ttfb`, `longTaskMs`, `islandWakeMs` (the slowest island's wake)                                                         |
| `record()`  | `maxInteractionMs`, `slowInteractions`, `errors`, `cls` (shifts with no input), `longTaskMs`, `failedRequests`, `serverBusyMs` (with `server: true`) |
| `profile()` | `score` (at least), `renderMs` (the median render), `jsBytes` (JS at start; a build only)                                                            |

`checkBudget(report, budget)` returns the same lines as data, for your own logic.

## Reports in CI

Every report a test takes is attached to its result as `ogygia.json`, which Playwright's HTML reporter shows and its JSON reporter carries. Two helpers turn two runs (a base branch and a head branch) into a PR comment:

```ts title=scripts/ogygia-comment.ts
import { compare, toMarkdown } from '@ogygia/testing';

// each run: the parsed `ogygia.json` attachments ({ test, reports }[])
const diff = compare(baseRun, headRun);
console.log(toMarkdown(diff)); // what moved, per test
```

For each test, the diff carries:

- **Findings:** new and fixed, by code.
- **Profiles:** score, server render and JS at start, with the files behind any change in JS.
- **Hydration:** each island instance that stopped or started hydrating cleanly (`Carousel#2: clean → failed`).
- **Sessions:** the counts that moved (errors, unexpected shifts, slow interactions, blocked navigations, the session's CLS).
- **Vitals:** only those that moved by more than a run's noise. The defaults are 150 ms for LCP and FCP, 100 ms for TTFB and 50 ms for INP (each also at least 10 % of the base value), and 0.02 for CLS. Set your own with `compare(baseRun, headRun, { noise: { lcp: 300 } })`.

### Several runs a side

Real pages are noisy, and comparing one run against one run turns that noise into false regressions, because a scripted session's errors, failed requests and interaction shifts depend on the path it happened to take. Give a test several runs per side, `{ test, runs: [reports, reports, reports] }`, and `compare` treats the signals differently:

- **Load signals** (page vitals and findings, hydration, and the profile's score, render time and JS) are compared on the median of the runs. A finding or a broken island counts when most runs have it.
- **Session signals** (the session's findings, errors, shifts, slow interactions and blocked navigations) count only when every head run has them and no base run does. A count moves only when every head run is above every base run, or every head run is below.

Three runs per side is a good starting point for the pages whose first pass shows a possible regression.

## Other runners and drivers

`createOgygia(driver)` gives the same four primitives over any driver that can run a function in the page (`evaluate`) and, for `profile()`, make a request (`get`). Puppeteer's `Page` works, for example. The matchers are plain `expect.extend` matchers:

```ts
import { expect } from 'vitest';
import { createOgygia, ogygiaMatchers } from '@ogygia/testing';

expect.extend(ogygiaMatchers);
const ogygia = createOgygia({ evaluate: (fn, arg) => page.evaluate(fn, arg) });
```

If you manage Playwright pages yourself without the fixture (for example two browser contexts in one test, one for a preview and one for a baseline), `playwrightDriver(page)` gives you the driver the fixture builds, `blockNavigation` included, and `turnOnDevtools(page, baseURL)` sets the cookie. `onNavigationHeld` is called each time a load is held back, so you can note which step of your script was running:

```ts
import { createOgygia, playwrightDriver, turnOnDevtools } from '@ogygia/testing';

await turnOnDevtools(previewPage, previewURL);
const preview = createOgygia(playwrightDriver(previewPage, { baseURL: previewURL, onNavigationHeld: (n) => log(step, n.url) }));
```
